# Performance rules

## The KPI workbook is a benchmark, not a target

- `BDC Digital KPI 2026.xlsx` (15 sheets, ~228K cells, ~48K formulas) is the reference file for timing. Use it to measure speedups. Do not write code for it.
- Never put sheet names, cell addresses, column indices, or formula shapes from one workbook into `Spark Grid Shared` or the app. That includes names like `"YTD Summary"`, `"Config"`, month names, `"dashboard"`, cells like `S5`, and column ranges like 17–24.
- Sheet order and evaluation order come from the formulas' real dependencies, never from sheet names.
- A fast path must work for any workbook with that formula pattern. If it only fires on the KPI file, it does not ship.
- This must print nothing for new or changed code:
  ```
  grep -rnE 'YTD|Ytd|ytd|KPI|"[Cc]onfig"|"[Dd]ashboard"' Packages/SparkGridCore/Sources "Spark Grid/State" "Spark Grid/Grid"
  ```
  Existing matches are debt. Delete them when you touch that code; do not add more.

## BugBashRunner is for debugging only

- `BugBashRunner` is a debug harness. Nothing the app needs may live only there, and nothing in it is evidence of what the user experiences.
- `recalculateEntireWorkbook` is not called by the app today. Speeding it up does not change open time, edit lag, or scrolling. Do not report it as a user-facing win.

## Diagnostics never run in live paths

- Diagnostics, probes, and profile logging go behind `#if DEBUG` and run only when a `FormulaRecalcProfile` is attached.
- Never add diagnostics to `displayValue`, `recalculate`, `cellsDidChange`, `ingest`, any `draw…` method, scroll handling, or document change tracking.
- Example of what not to do: the `YTD Summary!S5` probe at the end of `FormulaEngine.recalculate` runs once per lazily displayed cell and adds ~160 ms (Release) to the YTD tab's first screen.

## Measure what the user feels, in Release

- Time in a Release build (Product > Profile, or a Release scheme). Debug (`-Onone`) numbers are not comparable and do not count toward budgets.
- Profile first (Instruments Time Profiler or `sample`) and work on the largest cost. Do not optimize anything under ~5% of the path you are fixing.
- Time the paths the app runs, on the KPI file:
  1. Open: `SpreadsheetDocument.load` through the first painted grid.
  2. The unsaved-changes check (`SpreadsheetDocumentStore.computeDirtyState` / `WorkbookFingerprint`).
  3. First screen of each sheet, including `YTD Summary`.
  4. One cell edit, through `commitEdit` and the SwiftUI `onChange` sync.
  5. A tab switch (`selectSheet`).
  6. Save / autosave (`XLSXCodec.exportWorkbook`).
  7. Scrolling: the worst single screen while scrolling a month sheet's data rows.
- Also time at least one large workbook with a different shape. A change that helps only the KPI file is a regression.

## Main thread budget

- No work proportional to the whole workbook on the main thread per edit, selection change, scroll tick, or tab switch. That means no full-workbook hashing or encoding, no full export, and no rebuilding every sheet's values.
- Open, import, export, and autosave run off the main thread. The main thread gets a finished snapshot.
- Dirty tracking is a change counter, not a content hash. Switching tabs is not an edit.

## Answers must match

- The scalar `FormulaEvaluator` is the answer check. A faster path that disagrees with it does not ship.
- `YTD Summary!R5` fails to parse because the formula in the file is malformed (misplaced parentheses in the May term). A parse error is the correct result there. Do not special-case it.

## How to land a change

- One change per commit. Put the before and after Release timings for the affected paths from the list above in the commit message.
- If a change measures slower than the baseline, or fails the answer check, revert it. Do not stack another attempt on top of a path that already measured slower.
- No commits whose only purpose is adding logging to hot paths.

## Baseline (2026-10-11, KPI workbook, this Mac)

Measured by compiling `Spark Grid Shared` unmodified into a benchmark. Use these as the numbers to beat.

| Path | Release | Debug |
|---|---|---|
| `XLSXCodec.importWorkbook` | 6.1 s | 11.6 s |
| `WorkbookFingerprint` (each call; runs on open ×2, every edit, tab switch, save) | 3.7 s | 4.3 s |
| `XLSXCodec.exportWorkbook` (save / autosave) | 2.3 s | 2.8 s |
| One edit, `cellsDidChange` | 43 ms | 273 ms |
| `YTD Summary` first screen (60×25 cells) | 612 ms | 2,016 ms |
| June scroll, worst single screen | 63 ms | 300 ms |
| `recalculateEntireWorkbook` (not an app path) | 0.7 s | 3.3 s |
| Memory after import | ~1.15 GB | ~1.15 GB |

Where import time goes (Release, sampled): CoreXLSX worksheet decode ~42%, `expandSharedFormulas` pass ~32% (the file has no shared formulas), `importAutoFilter` regex ~5%, re-reading zip entries <0.1%.

## Known facts that change the plan

- Spark Grid's exporter writes formulas without their cached results (`<f>` with no `<v>`). Files saved by Spark Grid, including the KPI file, have no cached values, so "paint cached values first" does nothing for them until the exporter writes `<v>`.
- `WorkbookFingerprint` runs `JSONEncoder` once per cell format, for every cell, on every change. That is the edit lag.
- The grid's scroll planes are keyed on `selectionRevision`, so every click or arrow key discards them.
- `selectSheet` rebuilds the formula engine twice and discards computed values. The fingerprint includes the active tab, so a tab switch also marks the document edited.
